  "payload": {
    "evidence_contract": "judge_question:1",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-47361",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14273993/assets/fa3045b53d5a91ae7adbf450243338b988239749e4ae7340938943cce4c239fb.png)\n\nAn oil tank is leaking $q(t)$ liters of oil after $t$ hours.\n The graph of $q(t)$ is shown above.\n\nApproximate the rate at which the oil is leaking from the tank when $t=2$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{q(t+h)-q(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=2$ and step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{q(2+1)-q(2-1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{q(3)-q(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-2}{2} \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of leaking at the instant of time $t=2$ is $0.5\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}.$",
